<h1>API conventions</h1>
<p>Every engine service is a plain Lua table you import with <code>require</code>. This page explains how those tables are shaped, how results come back, how failures are reported, and which units the numbers use — read it once before diving into the individual APIs.</p>

<h2>What a service module is</h2>
<p>A service module is a small table of functions grouped by job (entities, transforms, audio, and so on). You import it into a local and call through that local. There are no engine globals: if you forget the <code>require</code>, the name is simply <code>nil</code> and the call fails loudly instead of silently doing something else.</p>
<pre><code>local Entity = require("demi.entity")
local Transform3D = require("demi.transform3d")

local id = Entity.find("skyforge_player")
local x, y, z = Transform3D.get_position(id)</code></pre>
<p><code>require("demi.entity")</code> returns the entity service table. <code>Entity.find("skyforge_player")</code> looks up an authored entity by its stable ID and returns its live handle, or <code>nil</code> when no entity has that name. <code>Transform3D.get_position(id)</code> returns three scalar numbers (the entity's local position) or three <code>nil</code> values when the entity has no transform. The local names (<code>Entity</code>, <code>Transform3D</code>) are yours to choose; the <code>"demi.…"</code> strings are the contract.</p>

<h2>Vector annotations</h2>
<p>When an API documents a vector type it uses the names <code>Vec2</code> and <code>Vec3</code>. Those are annotations for readers and editor tooling, not Lua classes: a <code>Vec3</code> is just three numbers. How you hold them depends on the call:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Transform getters and setters pass coordinates as <strong>separate scalar arguments and returns</strong>: <code>local x, y, z = Transform3D.get_position(id)</code>.</li>
  <li>Vectors embedded inside tables (spawn options, hit results, color-style fields) are <strong>fixed-length numeric arrays</strong>: <code>{ x, y }</code> or <code>{ x, y, z }</code>. Lua arrays start at index 1, so read them as <code>v[1]</code>, <code>v[2]</code>, <code>v[3]</code>.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>local crate = Entity.spawn("skyforge_crate", {
    position = { 4, 2, 0 },   -- Vec3 as a 3-number array
    velocity = { 1, 0, 0 },
})

local px, py, pz = Transform3D.get_position(crate)  -- three scalars</code></pre>
<p><code>Entity.spawn(name, options)</code> takes the entity name and an optional options table. <code>position</code> and <code>velocity</code> are <code>Vec3</code> numeric arrays (<code>index 1 = x</code>, <code>2 = y</code>, <code>3 = z</code>). The call returns the new entity handle, or <code>nil</code> if the name is unknown. The follow-up getter returns three scalars, not a table.</p>

<h2>How results come back</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Where it is used</th><th>How to read it</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>local x, y, z = …</code></td>
      <td>Transform position, rotation, scale</td>
      <td>One scalar per coordinate. If the entity has no transform, each coordinate is <code>nil</code> — check one of them before doing arithmetic.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>{ x, y }</code> / <code>{ x, y, z }</code></td>
      <td>Vectors inside tables (hits, spawn options, sizes)</td>
      <td>Numeric arrays of fixed length. Index from 1. A field documented as a vector is never a string like <code>"1,2,3"</code>.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>value, nil</code> or <code>nil, err</code></td>
      <td><code>Data.load</code>, <code>Data.parse_yaml</code>, HTTP requests</td>
      <td>Check the first result. Success is <code>value, nil</code>. Failure is <code>nil</code> plus an error table (shape below).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>nil</code></td>
      <td>Missing entity, unknown HUD node, missed raycast</td>
      <td>A single <code>nil</code> means “nothing found”. Guard with <code>if hit then … end</code> before reading fields.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>{}</code> (empty table)</td>
      <td>Bulk queries, children lists, path results from <code>Navigation2D</code></td>
      <td>An empty array means “the query ran and found nothing”, not an error. Iterate with <code>ipairs</code>.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Two pathfinding calls illustrate the split between <code>nil</code> and empty tables. <code>Grid.path</code> returns a path table on success and a single <code>nil</code> (plus a diagnostic you can log) when no path exists. <code>Navigation2D.path</code> always returns a table of waypoints — empty when nothing was found — and a diagnostic. Treat <code>nil</code> as “no result object”, and an empty table as “a result with zero items”.</p>
<pre><code>local Grid = require("demi.grid")
local Navigation2D = require("demi.navigation2d")

local gridPath = Grid.path("skyforge_astar", { 0, 0 }, { 5, 5 })
-- gridPath: array of {x, y} waypoints, or nil when unreachable (a diagnostic is logged)

local navPath = Navigation2D.path("skyforge_nav", { 0, 0 }, { 5, 5 })
-- navPath: always a table of waypoints (possibly empty) plus a diagnostic</code></pre>
<p><code>Grid.path(gridId, from, to)</code> takes grid coordinates as two-element arrays and returns either an array of waypoints or <code>nil</code>. <code>Navigation2D.path(navId, from, to)</code> returns a (possibly empty) waypoint array and a diagnostic table you can pass to <code>Debug.log</code>.</p>

<h2>Error shapes</h2>
<p>Calls that can fail for content reasons (bad data, missing files, network trouble) return <code>nil</code> plus a small error table instead of raising. The standard shape is:</p>
<pre><code>local Data = require("demi.data")

local value, err = Data.load("asset://data/skyforge_enemies")
if err then
    -- err.code    : short machine string, e.g. "not_found" or "schema_mismatch"
    -- err.message : human-readable sentence
    -- err.path    : JSON pointer into the document, or the URI
end</code></pre>
<p><code>Data.load(uri)</code> returns <code>value, nil</code> on success. On failure it returns <code>nil</code> and an error table with three string fields: <code>code</code> (stable, safe to branch on), <code>message</code> (safe to show or log), and <code>path</code> (where in the document the problem is, as a JSON pointer such as <code>/stats/0/hp</code>, or the requested URI when the whole document is missing).</p>

<h2>JSON null</h2>
<p>JSON has a real <code>null</code> value that is different from “field missing”. Lua only has <code>nil</code>, which also means “table key absent”, so DemiEngine exposes a sentinel instead:</p>
<pre><code>local Data = require("demi.data")

local value = Data.load("asset://data/skyforge_enemies")
print(Data.kind(value[1].nickname))   -- "null" when the field is JSON null
print(Data.is_null(value[1].nickname)) -- true

local written = { nickname = Data.null }</code></pre>
<p><code>Data.null</code> is the single sentinel value for JSON <code>null</code>. <code>Data.kind(value)</code> returns a string (<code>"null"</code>, <code>"number"</code>, <code>"string"</code>, <code>"boolean"</code>, <code>"array"</code>, <code>"object"</code>) describing what a loaded value is. <code>Data.is_null(value)</code> returns <code>true</code> only for the sentinel. Assign <code>Data.null</code> when you want to write a real null back out.</p>

<h2>Units</h2>
<ul>
  <li><code>Transform2D</code> rotation and <code>Transform3D</code> Euler rotation: <strong>radians</strong>.</li>
  <li>Angular velocity on rigidbodies: <strong>radians per second</strong>.</li>
  <li>Camera field of view: <strong>degrees</strong>.</li>
  <li>All <code>Time</code> values (<code>delta_time</code>, <code>fixed_time</code>, <code>time</code>): <strong>seconds</strong>.</li>
  <li>HUD positions and sizes: <strong>canvas units</strong> from the HUD document <code>canvas_size</code>, not raw screen pixels.</li>
</ul>

